🟢 2. SCALING
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Vertical Scaling
→ Make ONE server stronger.

Small Server
    ↓
Bigger Server
    ↓
Physical / Cost limit


Horizontal Scaling
→ Add MORE servers.

        ┌── Server 1
Client ─┼── Server 2
        └── Server 3

Why horizontal?
→ One machine has a ceiling.
→ Multiple machines provide more capacity + redundancy.


━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
🟢 3. LOAD BALANCER
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Problem:
Multiple servers exist.

Question:
→ Who decides where each request goes?

Solution:

Client
  ↓
Load Balancer
  ↓
S1   S2   S3

Load Balancer
→ Routes traffic to backend servers.
→ NOT application storage.
→ NOT business logic.

Algorithms:
→ Round Robin
→ Least Connections
→ IP Hash

Least Connections:
→ Send request to server with fewer active connections.


━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
🟢 4. HEALTH CHECKS
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

If:

S1 ✅
S2 ❌
S3 ✅

Load Balancer:
→ Detects unhealthy server.
→ Stops sending NEW requests to it.

Recovered server:
→ Health check first.
→ Then return to traffic.


Important:
Existing request on crashed server
→ may fail / timeout.

Future requests
→ go to healthy servers.


